  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nShowers and storms are decreasing over the area early this morning \nas the initial cold front of the Clipper low moving into Lake \nSuperior moves east out of our area, allowing us a quick reprieve \nfrom precipitation. However, don't expect this reprieve to last all \nthat long as wrap-around moisture moves in and restarts shower and \nthunderstorm activity across Upper MI. Thankfully, no strong \nthunderstorms are expected as the better severe weather threat will \nbe further south and east today. However, some heavy rainfall could \nbe seen at times, particularly over the east where the greatest \nrainfall totals are projected; this is even reflected in the latest \nNBM rainfall probabilities, with a 40-90% chance for greater than an \ninch of rainfall over the east between now and 8 PM EDT. As for the \nrest of the area, the chances for exceeding more than an inch of \nrainfall between now and 8 PM EDT this evening is no more than 30%, \nwith most spots in the central and west between 0-10%. In addition \nto the rain showers and non-severe thunderstorms, the high winds and \nwaves over Lake MI will come into contact with the Schoolcraft \nCounty shoreline, creating dangerous rip currents this morning into \nthis afternoon. Therefore, swimmers should avoid getting into Lake \nMI today. As the low lifts away from the area later today and CAA \nworks into the region tonight into Thursday, expect strong northwest \nwinds over Lake Superior to bring dangerous rip currents back to the \nshoreline of Alger County on Thursday. Simultaneously, while the \nmoisture aloft starts getting lost this evening, we could see some \nlake enhanced/upslope-forced light rain showers along and upslope \nfrom the Lake Superior shoreline. As high pressure builds into the \narea late this week, expect the rain showers to finally end across \nthe area sometime late tonight or on Thursday. \n\nOverall, expect near normal temperatures throughout the rest of this \nweek as highs stay within the 70s. Expect dry conditions by Friday \nas high pressure ridging builds in and becomes maximized at around \n1022mb over the Upper Great Lakes Friday morning. As we head into \nthe weekend towards next week, expect a troughing pattern to develop \nover the western CONUS. Because of this, the U.P. becomes set up \nwith Gulf air being funneled into the area, allowing warmer than \nnormal temperatures and more humid than normal air to return to the \narea. With shortwaves coming in from time to time, the earliest of \nwhich likely coming in either Saturday or Sunday, we could see some \nmuch-needed rainfall across the area for the latter half of the \nforecast period.",
